WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.020
Reading and math scores have long been
the primary measure of American

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:13.950
schooling. But how much do rising test
scores actually tell us about schools? A

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.960
lot less than we'd like!
In spring 2018, for instance, researchers

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.699
noted teacher impacts on test scores are
almost entirely uncorrelated with

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:29.189
teacher impacts on student classroom
behavior, attendance, truancy, and grades.

00:00:29.189 --> 00:00:33.480
Why might that be?
Well, when test scores go up, it can mean

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.750
that students are learning more, but it
can also mean that schools are spending

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.920
a lot of time on test preparation,
shifting attention from other subjects

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.360
to reading and math, or gaming the
statistics. If a pediatrician started

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.620
seeing an impressive number of patients
because she stopped doing thorough

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.940
examinations, we probably would not
celebrate her performance. That's because

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:59.070
it matters how she improves her stats. In
the same way, it matters how schools

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.800
raise their test scores. Higher scores
may mean that schools are getting better,

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.200
or that they're just pretending to do so.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.440
How much do you think test scores matter?

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.100
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us
know what other topics you'd like our

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.540
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be
sure to LIKE and subscribe for more

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.729
research and videos from AEI.

